raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does the local political climate change how strongly a person's political orientation is associated with COVID-19 vaccination attitudes and uptake in the United States? Previous research had largely examined individual orientation and local climate separately.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While previous research has identified the unique impact\nof both political orientation and political climate on vaccination attitudes, there has\nbeen little research on the interaction between personal political orientation and local\npolitical climate.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "The present research examines political climate as a potential moderator of the\nassociation between political orientation and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and\nuptake.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do the patterns for vaccination attitudes and uptake differ between the initial doses and subsequent boosters?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, by looking at both COVID vaccine and booster status and hesitancy, we\nidentify how attitudes and behaviors may have shifted as we transitioned from initial vaccine doses to boosters.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Regional political climate was more strongly associated with COVID-19 vaccine and booster hesitancy, and with reported booster uptake, among politically conservative people than among liberals. This difference by individual ideology did not extend to reported uptake of the initial vaccine.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "our findings indicate that political climate, as represented through regional 2020 presidential election votes,\nhas a stronger association with vaccine and booster hesitancy for individuals who are higher on political conservatism as\ncompared to those who are politically liberal. This same trend was observed for self-reported booster status, but not self-­\nreported vaccine status.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors used an online, self-reported survey of a nonprobability convenience sample recruited in the United States through Qualtrics Panels from March 8 to April 19, 2023.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This was an online self-reported study in which a nonprobability convenience sample\nwas recruited from the United States between March 8 and April 19, 2023.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Participants were a convenience sample recruited through Qualtrics Panels, an online research platform that manages\nparticipant recruitment and data quality for academic studies.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "This was a new analysis of data originally collected to study racial and socioeconomic marginalization in relation to COVID-19 vaccination attitudes and behaviors.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The data used in this study were part of a larger project assessing the impact of racial and socioeconomic marginalization on COVID-19 vaccine attitudes and behaviors [27]. The present research presents a novel analysis of the\ndataset from this project, focusing on political orientation and political climate, rather than marginalization.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Recruitment quotas called for equal numbers of White-only and Black-only participants, with half of each group below the $70,000 income threshold and no gender making up more than 60% of any race-by-income group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Half of the sample was requested to be White (and no other racial identity), and half the sample was requested\nto be Black (and no other racial identity). Within each racial group, half of the participants were requested to be below\nthe median annual income in the US [$70,000 [28]].",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, within each quota group it was requested that no more than 60% of the\nparticipants be of any gender.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Of 1,777 submissions, 970 failed Qualtrics quality screens, nine more were removed for illogical open-ended responses, and 115 lacked a valid ZIP code or matching election data, leaving 683 participants.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Qualtrics Panels collected data from 1777 participants, and identified 970 participants as not passing all quality screens\n(described above). We eliminated an additional nine participants whose open-ended responses did not make logical\nsense, resulting in a sample of 798 validated participants.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We eliminated an additional 115 participants who did not provide valid zip code data (N = 100) or whose zip code did not have corresponding election data (N = 15). The final sample\nfor these analyses included 683 participants.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Participants were 18 to 94 years old; 519 reported receiving the initial vaccine and 365 reported receiving at least one booster.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants ranged in age from 18 to 94 years (M = 46.29, SD = 18.94).",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "519 (76%) of participants reported receiving the initial COVID-19 vaccine and 365 (53%) reported\nreceiving at least one COVID-19 booster.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The individual conservatism score averaged two self-ratings, one on social issues and one on economic issues, each ranging from 0 (strongly liberal) to 10 (strongly conservative).",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "“social issues (for example, abortion, gun rights, gay rights)”",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "“economic issues (for example, taxation, government spending)”",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Both items were rated on a 11-point sliding scale from\nstrongly liberal (0) to strongly conservative (10). These two items were averaged to form a political conservatism scale.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Regional conservatism was measured as the Republican percentage of the 2020 presidential vote in each participant's ZIP code. The authors used New York Times election data, matched FIPS codes to ZIP codes, and averaged data across FIPS codes when more than one matched.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We assessed regional political climate using zip code data provided\nby participants and 2020 presidential election results sourced from the New York Times [22].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Political climate for each participant was determined by matching FIPS codes to corresponding zip\ncodes. In cases where a zip code included multiple FIPS codes, data was averaged across these FIPS.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Using the FIPS\ndata for each zip code, we calculated the percent of Republican votes by dividing the number of Republican votes by the\ntotal number of votes (multiplied by 100).",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Initial vaccination and booster uptake were measured with separate self-report questions, each allowing yes, no, or don't know.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Self-reported vaccine status and booster status were assessed through individual items, “have you received a COVID19 vaccine” and “have you received a COVID-19 booster”.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Possible\nresponses were yes, no, and I don’t know.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Vaccine hesitancy was measured with seven five-point items adapted from an adult vaccination attitudes scale to refer specifically to COVID-19. Booster hesitancy was measured with items from a 2022 booster survey.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Vaccine hesitancy (i.e., negative attitudes toward vaccination) was measured\nusing the seven items from the Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale [24], which assesses the perceived value of\nvaccines in general. We modified the items to assess attitudes specifically toward the COVID-19 vaccine",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Each item was rated on a 5-point Likert scale ranging from disagree\nstrongly (1) to agree strongly (5). These items were averaged to form a vaccine hesitancy scale. Booster hesitancy was\nassessed using items from a 2022 survey on COVID-19 booster hesitancy [25].",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors ran confirmatory factor analyses on both sets of hesitancy items. Both scales had a Cronbach's alpha of .93, and the items were averaged within each scale.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We conducted confirmatory factor analysis on the vaccine and booster hesitancy items (S2 File). The vaccine hesitancy items demonstrated adequate reliability (α = .93, 95% CI [.92,.93]) and were\naveraged to form the vaccine hesitancy scale.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "The booster hesitancy items demonstrated adequate reliability (α = .93, 95%\nCI [.93,.94]) and were averaged to form the booster hesitancy scale.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The regressions controlled for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access. Perceived access was measured by asking respondents how easily they could get a vaccine or booster if needed.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "controlling for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Perceived vaccine access was assessed using a single-item question “If I needed to get a COVID-19 vaccine\nor booster, I could easily get it”, rated on a 5-point Likert scale from Disagree Strongly (1) to Agree Strongly (5).",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The bivariate analyses used Pearson correlations for non-nominal variables and t-tests and chi-square tests for group comparisons involving hesitancy or uptake.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Pearson correlations were used to assess\ncorrelations between all non-nominal variables (i.e., age, income, education, perceived vaccine access, vaccine and\nbooster hesitancy, political conservatism, and conservative regional climate).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Mean differences in categorical predictors (i.e., race and gender) across booster status and vaccine status were assessed using t-tests, and differences in\nfrequencies of vaccine status and booster status by gender and race were assessed using X2.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Independent samples\nt-tests were used to assess differences in all non-nominal variables across vaccinated and unvaccinated participants.\nThe same analyses were run to assess differences between boosted and unboosted participants.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors used ordinary least squares for hesitancy and logistic models for uptake. For each outcome, they fit a controls-only model, added individual conservatism and regional climate, then added their interaction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Multiple linear regression models were built to assess the association between political conservatism and conservative regional climate with\nvaccine hesitancy, controlling for age, gender, education, income, race, and perceived vaccine access. Ordinary least\nsquares estimation was used with default standard errors.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "same process was used with booster hesitancy as the outcome variable to assess the association between political conservatism and conservative regional climate with booster hesitancy",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Additionally, we ran two sets of generalized linear models with logit link\nand model-­based standard errors predicting vaccine status and booster status from the same three models described\nabove.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Model 1 included only control variables age, gender, education, income, race, and vaccine access",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Model 2 included control variables and political conservatism and conservative regional climate. Model 3 included all variables from Model 2 and the interaction between\npolitical conservatism and conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analyses excluded cases with missing data on the variables used, were conducted in R 4.4.1, and used a two-sided significance threshold of .05.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For all analyses, any participants with missing data on any of these variables were excluded from the analysis.\nAll analysis and data visualization was conducted with R version 4.4.1 [29]. Two-sided p-values less than 0.05 were\nconsidered statistically significant.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "In adjusted models, greater individual conservatism and a more conservative regional climate were each associated with greater vaccine and booster hesitancy and lower reported uptake of both.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Political conservatism and conservative regional climate were both associated with higher levels of COVID-19 vaccine and booster hesitancy and lower rates of self-reported vaccination and boosting, when controlling for age, education, income, and race.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Hesitancy about the initial vaccine was low among liberals regardless of regional climate. It rose with individual conservatism, and the increase was steeper in more conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The interaction for vaccine hesitancy indicates that at the lowest levels of conservatism (i.e., liberalism) vaccine hesitancy is lower regardless of regional climate.\nAs political conservatism increases vaccine hesitancy also increases, but this increase is even greater for individuals\nwho live in a more conservative regional climate.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Conservative participants were more hesitant about boosters in conservative regions than in liberal ones. Among the most liberal participants, booster hesitancy was slightly lower in more conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "There was a similar pattern for booster hesitancy, with more politically\nconservative individuals in a conservative regional climate having higher booster hesitancy than conservative individuals\nin a liberal regional climate. For the most politically liberal individuals, booster hesitancy was slightly lower in more conservative regional climates.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The predicted probability of reporting a booster was above 65% for the most liberal participants regardless of climate. For the most conservative participants it was about 60% in liberal regions and below 35% in conservative regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate. The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less\nthan 35% likely to get boosted (OR =.83, p = .026).",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The interaction between individual conservatism and regional climate was not statistically significant for reported receipt of the initial vaccine.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The interaction between political conservatism\nand conservative regional climate (Table 6) was not significant (OR = 1.02, p = .833).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "Because ZIP-code voting is only a proxy for local political climate, the study cannot rule out other regional factors, such as population density, as explanations for the associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that\nother zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper. Regional factors (e.g., population\ndensity) may play a role in linking 2020 election votes to vaccine attitudes and behaviors.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Adjusting for demographics did not establish that the associations were the same across demographic groups. The pooled estimates could mask subgroup differences or aggregation effects such as Simpson's paradox.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While our analyses controlled\nfor demographic covariates commonly associated with vaccine hesitancy, we did not formally assess effect modification\nby these factors. Consequently, the reported pooled associations should be interpreted as average effects across the\nsample, and we cannot exclude the possibility that heterogeneity across demographic subgroups could produce aggregation effects such as Simpson’s paradox.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Reported vaccination status may not reflect actual status because respondents may misremember or give socially desirable answers.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Self-report is susceptible to social desirability [30] and recall bias [31], and we cannot assume that reported vaccine\nstatus reflects actual vaccine status.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The sample included only people with access to the online recruitment platform and only White and Black panelists, limiting generalizability to the broader U.S. population.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, our sample was recruited through an online platform and only includes\nparticipants with access to this platform. Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through\nQualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional design cannot establish causality or the direction of the associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Finally, it should be noted that this\ndata is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study did not test what accounts for the booster pattern. Social pressure is one possibility, and the data do not distinguish a stronger influence of liberal environments from a greater responsiveness to local environments among conservatives.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While we did not asses the mechanism through which this occurs, these\nfindings suggest that environmental factors (e.g., social pressure) may impact decisions to receive annual COVID-19\ndoses.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "The interaction between political climate and\norientation suggests that liberal environments may exert more influence on conservative individuals than conservative\nenvironments exert on liberal individuals. It could also be the case that conservative individuals are more influenced by\ntheir environments than their liberal counterparts.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend empirical research on politics and COVID-19 vaccination. They retain its distinction between personal political orientation and regional political climate but bring the two together to test an interaction rather than treating them separately.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our research is consistent with previous findings that conservative political orientation and a conservative\npolitical climate are both associated with COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and decreased likelihood of receiving a COVID-19\nvaccine [6,7,10,19]. Previous studies have focused on either political orientation or political climate.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "By looking at political\norientation and region climate, we identify a novel and important finding, specifically that conservative individuals are more\nlikely to received booster doses in liberal regions.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Rönn, Menzies, and Salomon found that counties and states with higher Democratic vote shares in the 2020 presidential election had higher COVID-19 vaccination rates in 2021–2022. The present paper builds on that regional association by asking whether it varies with a person's own ideology.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For example, at both the county and the state level there was\na positive association between Democratic vote share (i.e., % of people voting for the\nDemocratic candidate) in the 2020 presidential election and COVID-19 vaccination\nrates from 2021−2022 [19].",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
